<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Paz, Baja California Sur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a> is a port city on the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and the capital of the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baja-california">Baja California</a> Sur. Spanish attempts to settle its bay began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in 1535. In November 1853 the American filibuster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> landed there and proclaimed the Republic of Lower California, before moving north and abandoning the venture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-paz-baja-california-sur.jpg" alt="La Paz, Baja California Sur" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ProtoplasmaKid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_a%C3%A9rea_del_Museo_de_Arte_de_Baja_California_Sur_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a> lies on the Bay of La Paz on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baja-california">Baja California</a> peninsula, near the Sierra de la Laguna, which has more than 100 endemic plants. It has a hot desert climate with little rain, and in summer the Coromuel winds blow at night from the Pacific over the peninsula into the bay. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> population was 250,141 in 2020, nearly a third of the state's population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first European known to have landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baja-california">Baja California</a> was Fortun Ximenez. In 1533 Hernan Cortes sent two ships to explore the Pacific. On the Concepcion, Ximenez led a revolt in which the captain, Diego de Becerra, was killed, then sailed to the bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a>, believing it an island. The Spaniards saw the large pearls the local people extracted from oysters in the bay, plundered them and raped the women. The violence led to a confrontation in which Ximenez and some of his companions died.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> led a third expedition himself and on 3 May 1535 reached the bay he named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> de la Santa Cruz, where he took possession and planned a colony. The transport ships bringing supplies were lost in a storm, and Cortes left to find food. Francisco de Ulloa was left in command, but complaints from relatives of the colonists led the viceroy to order the colony abandoned. An enemy of Cortes then gave the territory the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, drawn from a novel of the time, perhaps to insult him.</p><p>The city was founded in 1720. Between 1768 and 1803 Franciscan missionaries established political and religious jurisdiction over Baja California Sur, shaping settlement and relations with Indigenous people. In the mid-19th century the American filibuster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a> the capital of his short-lived venture, which collapsed through lack of US support and pressure from the Mexican government to retake the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a> is the capital and largest city of the Mexican state of Baja California Sur. Its economy rests on tourism, especially eco-tourism around the Isla Espiritu Santo group, as well as fishing, agriculture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mining. It is home to several marine biology institutes, among them CIBNOR and CICIMAR. The Regional Museum of Anthropology and History, opened in 1981, shows fossils, archaeology and replicas of rock paintings. John Steinbeck visited in 1940 and described the town in The Pearl.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Paz, Baja California Sur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William Walker, a Tennessee-born adventurer, sailed from San Francisco with about 45 men and landed at <b>La Paz</b> in November 1853.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-lower-california">Republic of Lower California</a></li></ul></section>
